subQuestions.questionText:
Plants do not have nervous systems, yet they are still able to detect and respond to changes in their environment. They do this through specialised plant hormones (chemical messengers) that are produced in tiny amounts in the growing regions of the plant.

Describe how plants respond to TWO important environmental stimuli — light and gravity — by referring to:
• the role of auxins in shoots and the meaning of phototropism and geotropism;
• how a shoot bends towards a unidirectional source of light, with full reference to the action of auxins;
• the response of a root to gravity, with reference to auxin distribution;
• the use of a clinostat (DIAGRAM D) to investigate the effect of gravity on plant growth — describe what you would expect to see in setup A and setup B after 24 hours and explain why;
• TWO practical applications of plant hormones in commercial agriculture.

Your response should be in the form of a coherent piece of writing using scientific terminology. Where appropriate, fully labelled drawings or annotated tables may be included to support your answer.

(Refer to DIAGRAM D in the question paper — clinostat experiment investigating geotropism in seedlings.)
